   {
    "id": "i16",
    "kind": "Javadoc",
    "lines": [
     166,
     179
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Tests that a template beginning with the two-character escape sequence {@code $$} followed by a variable expression results in a single literal {@code $} concatenated with the unexpanded variable expression. <p>The default escape character in {@link StringSubstitutor} is {@code $}. When the substitutor encounters {@code $$}, it treats the first {@code $} as escaping the second, producing one literal {@code $} and consuming the escape pair without expanding anything. The variable expression that follows ({@code ${…}}) is then encountered fresh and is expanded normally — but because the escape already consumed the leading pair, what actually arrives at the substitutor is {@code $${var}} (two dollars then the expression), which is resolved as one literal {@code $} followed by the expanded variable, giving {@code $}{value}.</p> <p>Concretely, {@code $$${a}} (3 dollar signs) is reduced to {@code $${a}} (2 dollar signs + literal {@code {a}}) — the escape \"converts\" 3 leading dollars into 2, leaving the inner variable unexpanded.</p>"
   },
   {
    "id": "i17",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     182,
     183
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "\"$$${a}\" → \"$${a}\": the leading \"$$\" is the escape sequence for a literal \"$\", and the immediately following \"${a}\" is then treated as a literal (not substituted)."
   },
   {
    "id": "i18",
    "kind": "LineComment",
    "lines": [
     186,
     186
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Same escaping behaviour applies regardless of variable-name length."
   }
  ]
